, '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\n2: 1, A: 1, Q: 3, J: 3, 10: 4, 9: 3, 8: 2, 7: 3, 6: 4, 5: 1, 4: 1, 3: 2\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♥A\nsingle: ♥2\nsingle: BlackJoker\nsingle: RedJoker\nrocket: BlackJoker RedJoker\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': "**Analyzing the Current Turn: A Peasant's Perspective**\n\nOkay, here's the situation. I'm playing as a peasant. My teammate just played a King (club K). The Landlord is the next player after me. My hand is loaded with a Rocket, pairs of 2s, Aces, and Kings, plus a couple of smaller pairs and some singles. I need to make the optimal play.\n\nMy initial thought is to evaluate the board state. My teammate just played a King. I have strong cards that can beat the King but, the Landlord is sitting right after me in this round. The Landlord has played high singles before. This means I need to think strategically.\n\nLet's do some card counting. I've played a Queen, and the Landlord just played a Jack. I've got two of each Ace, two, and King. The King my teammate just played is the card to beat. If I beat the King, I am obligated to play a high card from my hand (A, 2, or Joker) and potentially break my high pairs (Ace, or 2s) or Rocket, which is bad. Is it better to let the Landlord or Teammate win this trick?\n\nFrom my perspective, there's only one logical move: Pass. My teammate played a strong card, which in most cases, I should let the Landlord struggle against. My teammate can only be beat by Aces, 2s, or a Joker. In this case, there's a good chance the Landlord will be forced to use a high card in response. This will keep my high cards to control the game later.\n\nLet's double-check the history to be sure. My teammate (Left player) and I are on the same team. My history of playing cards should be aligned to make sure that I'm optimizing this strategic thinking.\n\nMy team, we win when the Landlord is forced to pass. Pass is the play here.\n\nIf I use my Ace or 2, I may break my pairs. My Rocket is a top card as well. Breaking the high cards is a bad strategy in general. The Landlord has high cards to potentially respond. We want to force the Landlord to play his high cards first.\n\nWith this strategic play, I should pass. This keeps the cards where they should be and helps us win overall.\n\nLet's recheck the rules to confirm that passing is legal. Okay. I am not in a free play. I can pass. This reinforces the rightness of my pass strategy.\n\nIn summary, the best move is to pass.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: My hand is extremely strong, featuring a Rocket (Red Joker + Black Joker), and pairs of 2s, Aces, and Kings. My teammate has just played a King. Since the King is a high-ranking card, only an Ace, 2, or Joker can beat it. There is only one unknown Ace and one unknown 2 remaining in the deck. If I beat my teammate\'s King, I would have to break my top pairs (AA or 22) or my Rocket, which drastically reduces my hand\'s power and flexibility. Furthermore, beating my teammate relieves the pressure on the Landlord, who acts next. I should allow the Landlord to attempt to beat the King; if he does (using the last Ace or 2), I can easily regain control with my remaining high cards. If he passes, my teammate retains the lead.\n\nReason: My teammate played a strong card (King). Beating it would require breaking my top pairs (AA or 22) or Rocket, which is inefficient and counterproductive as it clears the way for the Landlord. Passing forces the Landlord to spend high cards or yield control to my teammate.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
